WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.238
Who owns all this?

2
00:00:01.238 --> 00:00:06.538
Chapter 08's rule is one decider per
row, and it starts from FedRAMP's split.

3
00:00:06.538 --> 00:00:10.050
The provider owns the service
boundary and its certification.

4
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.932
The agency owns the
boundary of its own system,

5
00:00:12.932 --> 00:00:16.725
and everything it builds,
configures and connects inside it.

6
00:00:16.725 --> 00:00:20.475
On the agency side, the
authorizing official determines the

7
00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:24.075
authorization boundary, with
input from the system owner.

8
00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:27.725
That comes straight from NIST SP 800-37.

9
00:00:27.725 --> 00:00:32.290
The official signs every documented
exception, approves the plan of action

10
00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:36.637
and milestones, and decides whether
a change requires reauthorization.

11
00:00:36.637 --> 00:00:39.343
Explicit risk acceptance
can't be delegated,

12
00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:43.950
and for federal agencies the role is
assigned to government personnel only.

13
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.139
A designated representative
can run the process;

14
00:00:47.139 --> 00:00:49.550
only the authorizing official signs.

15
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.978
The system owner is the boundary owner:

16
00:00:51.978 --> 00:00:54.862
keeper of the boundary
record and the package.

17
00:00:54.862 --> 00:00:59.524
The CISO sets the control requirements
and decides what counts as evidence,

18
00:00:59.524 --> 00:01:02.874
and the ISSO reads provider
notices against the record.

19
00:01:02.874 --> 00:01:06.464
The sponsor and CIO choose
the clouds, fund the platform,

20
00:01:06.464 --> 00:01:09.896
set the sequence and break
the ties, which will happen,

21
00:01:09.896 --> 00:01:13.563
because everyone can reasonably
claim the landing zone.

22
00:01:13.563 --> 00:01:15.250
Then the people who do the work.

23
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:20.067
The platform team builds the landing
zone, the policy and subscription vending,

24
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.987
but it doesn't decide
whether a gap is acceptable.

25
00:01:22.987 --> 00:01:25.224
That goes to the authorizing official.

26
00:01:25.224 --> 00:01:28.812
Workload owners declare the
features their designs depend on.

27
00:01:28.812 --> 00:01:31.649
Security operations runs drift detection.

28
00:01:31.649 --> 00:01:33.751
And acquisition holds the contract,

29
00:01:33.751 --> 00:01:37.812
because only a contracting officer
can change what the agency bought.

30
00:01:37.812 --> 00:01:40.487
FedRAMP is consulted, not in charge.

31
00:01:40.487 --> 00:01:42.706
Agencies notify it after authorizing,

32
00:01:42.706 --> 00:01:46.937
and work with it when a determination
conflicts with a provider's package.

33
00:01:46.937 --> 00:01:51.076
Its certification is an input to
the authorizing official's decision.

34
00:01:51.076 --> 00:01:52.610
It never replaces it.
